# Important notes

## 1. Capacity usage
Because one slot is reserved to distinguish full from empty, a buffer of `Capacity` stores at most `Capacity - 1` elements.

If you want exactly `N` usable slots, allocate `N + 1`.

---

## 2. Type requirements
This version uses placement new and explicit destruction, so it supports non-trivial `T`.

The destructor currently does:

```cpp
T tmp;
while (pop(tmp)) {}
```

That requires `T` to be default-constructible, which is undesirable.

A better destructor avoids that:

```cpp
~spsc_ring_buffer() {
    std::size_t head = head_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
    const std::size_t tail = tail_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
    while (head != tail) {
        slot_ptr(head)->~T();
        head = increment(head);
    }
}
```

That version is better. Here is the improved full class:

